    "description": "Job Purpose To inspect, diagnose, maintain, repair, and troubleshoot electrical and electronic systems of company vehicles, trucks, machinery, and other automotive equipment to ensure their proper functioning, reliability, safety, and availability for operation.The Auto Electrician is responsible for identifying electrical faults, conducting preventive maintenance, repairing electrical components, and ensuring that all electrical maintenance activities are performed according to established technical standards, safety procedures, and company regulations. 2. Main Duties and Responsibilities 2.1 Automotive Electrical Inspection and Diagnosis Inspect vehicle electrical and electronic systems to identify faults, defects, and potential problems. Diagnose electrical problems in company vehicles, trucks, and other automotive equipment. Identify faults in electrical wiring, circuits, switches, relays, fuses, and connectors. Read and interpret automotive electrical diagrams, wiring schematics, and technical manuals. Use appropriate diagnostic tools and testing equipment to identify electrical system problems. Investigate electrical failures and recommend appropriate corrective measures. 2.2 Electrical Repair and Maintenance Repair and maintain vehicle electrical systems and components. Repair, replace, and install defective electrical wires, connectors, switches, and electrical accessories. Maintain and repair vehicle starting systems, including starter motors and related components. Inspect, test, maintain, and repair charging systems, including alternators and voltage regulators. Inspect and maintain vehicle batteries, battery terminals, and electrical connections. Repair and maintain headlights, indicators, brake lights, horns, dashboard instruments, and other electrical accessories. Maintain vehicle ignition systems and related electrical components. Repair electrical faults in vehicle control systems and electronic components. Perform electrical repairs in accordance with manufacturer specifications and approved maintenance procedures. 2.3 Preventive Maintenance Conduct regular inspection of vehicle electrical systems according to the preventive maintenance schedule. Check battery condition, charging performance, electrical connections, and wiring systems. Inspect electrical components to identify signs of damage, overheating, corrosion, or malfunction. Perform routine electrical maintenance to prevent unexpected vehicle breakdowns. Recommend replacement of worn-out or defective electrical components. Ensure that vehicles are electrically safe and ready for operation. Report recurring electrical problems and recommend long-term corrective solutions. 2.4 Electrical Installation and Equipment Maintenance Install automotive electrical accessories and equipment as required. Maintain vehicle electrical harnesses and wiring systems. Install and maintain electrical components in accordance with technical specifications. Assist in the installation and maintenance of vehicle monitoring systems, alarms, and other approved electrical devices. Ensure proper connection, insulation, and functioning of installed electrical components. Test electrical installations before releasing vehicles for operation. 2.5 Workshop Tools and Equipment Management Properly use multimeters, battery testers, diagnostic scanners, test lamps, and other electrical testing equipment. Maintain electrical tools and workshop equipment in good working condition. Ensure proper handling, storage, and protection of tools and equipment. Report damaged, defective, or missing tools and equipment to the immediate supervisor. Maintain cleanliness and orderliness of the electrical maintenance work area. 2.6 Safety and Environmental Protection Follow company occupational health and safety rules and workshop safety procedures. Use appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) during electrical maintenance activities. Ensure that electrical systems are safely isolated before carrying out repairs. Prevent electrical short circuits, fire hazards, and damage to vehicle components. Handle batteries and electrical materials safely and according to approved procedures. Maintain proper housekeeping in the workshop. Report unsafe working conditions, electrical hazards, and accidents immediately. Follow approved procedures for handling and disposing of used batteries, wires, and electrical waste. 2.7 Documentation and Reporting Record all electrical inspections, diagnostic findings, repairs, and maintenance activities. Prepare maintenance reports indicating identified faults, corrective actions, and materials used. Maintain accurate records of electrical spare parts and materials consumed. Report vehicle electrical problems requiring major repair or replacement. Inform the immediate supervisor about completed maintenance work and vehicle readiness. Maintain proper documentation of vehicle electrical maintenance history. 2.8 Spare Parts and Materials Management Identify required electrical spare parts and maintenance materials. Request necessary spare parts and materials through the approved procedure. Ensure proper utilization of electrical spare parts and materials. Verify the suitability and quality of electrical components before installation. Report shortages, defective parts, and unnecessary material consumption to the supervisor. 2.9 Other Responsibilities Work collaboratively with mechanics, drivers, maintenance technicians, and other relevant employees. Provide technical support during vehicle inspection and maintenance activities. Participate in technical training and skill development programs. Perform other related duties assigned by the immediate supervisor within the scope of the position.",
